Pmod MicroSD (Digilent) on Nucleo-F411RE
========================================

This overlay enables the Digilent Pmod MicroSD (microSD card slot) over SPI
on the Nucleo-F411RE using the Arduino Uno R3 connector.

Pin wiring (Pmod MicroSD 12-pin J1 → Nucleo-F411RE Arduino connector)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

| Pmod pin | Signal  | Nucleo-F411RE       | Arduino name |
|----------|---------|---------------------|--------------|
| 1        | ~CS     | PB6                 | D10          |
| 2        | MOSI    | PA7                 | D11          |
| 3        | MISO    | PA6                 | D12          |
| 4        | SCK     | PA5                 | D13          |
| 5        | GND     | GND                 | GND          |
| 6        | VCC     | 3.3V                | 3.3V         |

Pins 7–12 (DAT1, DAT2, CD, NC, GND, VCC) are not required for SPI mode;
leave them unconnected or connect GND/VCC if your cable has them.

Build
-----

Using the filesystem sample (recommended). From your Zephyr workspace root
(the directory that contains zephyr/ and west.yml):

  cd <path-to-your-zephyr-workspace>
  west build -b nucleo_f411re zephyr/samples/subsys/fs/fs_sample --build-dir build_nucleo_f411re -- \
    -DDTC_OVERLAY_FILE=boards/nucleo_f411re_pmod_microsd.overlay \
    -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE=boards/nucleo_f411re_pmod_microsd.conf

Using the overlay from the board directory in your own app (enable CONFIG_SPI
in your prj.conf or a .conf). From your Zephyr workspace root:

  cd <path-to-your-zephyr-workspace>
  west build -b nucleo_f411re --build-dir build_nucleo_f411re -- -DDTC_OVERLAY_FILE=zephyr/boards/st/nucleo_f411re/pmod_microsd.overlay

Flash (macOS)
-------------

From your Zephyr workspace root, use the same build directory as above:

  cd <path-to-your-zephyr-workspace>
  west flash -d build_nucleo_f411re --runner openocd

See the log (macOS)
-------------------

The board uses USART2 at 115200 8N1 over the ST-Link Virtual COM port. Connect
the Nucleo via the ST-LINK USB port, then:

1. Find the serial port:

     ls /dev/cu.usb*

   You should see something like /dev/cu.usbmodem14103.

2. Open a serial terminal at 115200 baud. For example with screen:

     screen /dev/cu.usbmodem14103 115200

   Replace the device name with yours. To exit screen: Ctrl+A, then K, then Y.

3. Reset the board (USER button or unplug/replug USB) to see the sample output
   (e.g. block count, sector size, "Disk mounted.", and the root directory
   listing).

Required Kconfig (usually enabled by the sample)
------------------------------------------------

- CONFIG_DISK_ACCESS
- CONFIG_SDHC
- CONFIG_SPI
- For FAT FS: CONFIG_FILE_SYSTEM and CONFIG_FAT_FILESYSTEM_ELM

Usage (init, read, write)
-------------------------

The SD card is exposed as a disk named "SD" via the Disk Access API. You can:

1. Initialize: disk_access_init() or DISK_IOCTL_CTRL_INIT.
2. Read/write blocks: disk_access_read(), disk_access_write().
3. Or mount a filesystem (e.g. FAT) and use fs_open(), fs_read(), fs_write().

See Zephyr documentation: Disk Access API, File system API, and
samples/subsys/fs/fs_sample for a full example.

